It's not usually a good idea to create a factor with just two indicators as the solution can often be unstable, and unless there are other variables in the model (e.g. an external correlate or SNP) the model is unidentified without adding in an extra constraint, e.g. that the loadings are equal. If the genetic correlation is .12 then a model in which the standardized loadings are equal should produce a standardized estimate of sqrt(.12) for each loading. I'm not sure whether that tells you much beyond knowing the rg though.
I can't say why your model isn't converging given that you haven't supplied the syntax, but I would guess that there is an extra parameter being estimated, or a missing constraint. I would also recommend looking back on previous chains on this forum, as related questions regarding two indicator factor models have come up before. Finally, I often recommend this paper which discusses many relevant concepts within SEM: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02361160. See Figure 3 ii for the model you are interested in.
Elliot

